## Foundational Concepts

### Scaling Systems

- __Vertical Scaling__: Adding more resources to a single machine (RAM, CPU, SSD)

  - Pros: Faster (intra-process calls), consistent (single machine), simpler architecture
  - Cons: Hardware limitations, single point of failure, expensive

- __Horizontal Scaling__: Adding more machines to distribute load

  - Pros: Theoretically unlimited scaling, better fault tolerance, cost-effective
  - Cons: More complex architecture, network calls (slower), data consistency challenges

- Real-world systems typically use a combination of both approaches

### CAP Theorem

- In a distributed system, you can have only 2 out of 3:

  - __Consistency__: All nodes see the same data at the same time
  - __Availability__: Every request gets a response (success or failure)
  - __Partition Tolerance__: System continues working despite network failures

- Example trade-offs:

  - Banking systems prioritize consistency over availability
  - Social media platforms often prioritize availability over consistency

### Capacity Estimation

- Crucial for system design planning
- Involves calculating storage, memory, and processing needs
- Example: YouTube daily storage = Users × Upload Rate × Average Size × Redundancy Factor

## Network & Data Transfer

### Network Protocols

- __IP Protocol__: Basic addressing and routing of packets
- __TCP__: Connection-oriented protocol ensuring reliable delivery
- __UDP__: Connectionless protocol favoring speed over reliability (used in streaming)
- __HTTP/HTTPS__: Application layer protocol for web communication
- __DNS Resolution Process__: How domain names are converted to IP addresses

### Process vs Thread

- __Process__: Independent program execution with its own memory space

- __Thread__: Smallest unit of CPU execution within a process, shares memory

- __Key Differences__:

  - Creating processes is more resource-intensive than threads
  - Threads share memory space, processes don't
  - Context switching between threads is faster
  - Inter-thread communication is more efficient than inter-process

## Data Storage

### Databases

- __Relational Databases (RDBMS)__:

  - Structured data in tables with relationships
  - ACID compliance (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability)
  - Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle
  - Use cases: Banking systems, e-commerce platforms

- __NoSQL Databases__:

  - Schema-less data models
  - Types: Key-value, document, columnar, graph
  - Prioritize scalability and performance over strict consistency
  - Examples: MongoDB, Cassandra, Redis, Neo4j
  - Use cases: Social media, content delivery, real-time analytics

### Database Optimization Techniques

#### Indexing

- __B-tree Indexes__: Most common, balances search/insert/delete operations
- __Geospatial Indexes__: For location-based queries
- __Inverted Indexes__: For text search and pattern matching

#### Sharding

- Horizontally partitioning data across multiple database instances

- Strategies:

  - Range-based: Data divided by value ranges
  - Hash-based: Using hash function to distribute evenly
  - Directory-based: Using lookup service to locate data

#### Bloom Filters

- Probabilistic data structure to test if element is in a set
- Used to prevent "one-hit wonders" from being stored in cache
- Can give false positives but never false negatives

## Caching

### Caching Strategies

- __Write-through__: Update both cache and main memory simultaneously

  - Pros: Data consistency, simple implementation
  - Cons: Higher latency for writes, increased memory traffic

- __Write-back__: Update cache first, write to memory later when needed

  - Pros: Lower write latency, reduced memory traffic
  - Cons: Risk of data loss, more complex implementation

### Cache Eviction Policies

- __LRU (Least Recently Used)__: Discards least recently accessed items first
- __LFU (Least Frequently Used)__: Discards least frequently accessed items
- __FIFO (First In, First Out)__: Discards oldest items first
- __Sliding Window__: Considers both recency and frequency

### Content Delivery Networks (CDNs)

- Distributed network of servers to deliver content closer to users
- Improves load times by reducing distance between user and content
- Reduces load on origin servers

## System Architecture

### Load Balancing

- Distributes incoming network traffic across multiple servers

- Algorithms:

  - Round Robin: Sequential distribution
  - Least Connections: Sends to server with fewest active connections
  - IP Hash: Uses client IP to determine server (session persistence)

- __Consistent Hashing__: Minimizes remapping when servers are added/removed

  - Maps servers and requests to points on a hash ring
  - When a server is added/removed, only a fraction of keys need remapping
  - Uses multiple virtual nodes per server for better distribution

### Microservices vs Monoliths

- __Monolithic Architecture__:

  - Single, self-contained application
  - Pros: Simpler development, testing, deployment; efficient communication
  - Cons: Scaling challenges, technology lock-in, longer deployment cycles

- __Microservices Architecture__:

  - Collection of small, independent services
  - Pros: Independent scaling, fault isolation, technology flexibility
  - Cons: Increased complexity, network overhead, distributed data management

