## Advanced Concepts

### Distributed Concurrency

- __Database Locking__: Prevents multiple transactions from modifying same data

  - Pessimistic: Locks data before access
  - Optimistic: Checks for conflicts at commit time

- __Two-Phase Locking (2PL)__:

  - Growing phase: Acquire all needed locks
  - Shrinking phase: Release locks, no new acquisitions

- __Leaky Bucket Model (Queue-based Serialization)__:

  - Places requests in a queue for sequential processing
  - Guarantees order and atomicity
  - Trade-off: Higher latency

### Data Consistency Models

- __Linearizable/Serializable__:

  - Strongest consistency model
  - All operations appear to execute atomically in some sequential order
  - High consistency, but lower performance

- __Eventual Consistency__:

  - System may return stale data temporarily
  - All replicas eventually converge to the same state
  - Higher performance, but temporary inconsistencies

- __Causal Consistency__:

  - If operation A causally affects B, all nodes see A before B
  - Stronger than eventual, weaker than linearizable
  - Good balance of performance and consistency

- __Quorum-based Consistency__:

  - Uses majority voting to ensure consistency
  - Write quorum (W) + Read quorum (R) > Total replicas (N)
  - Tunable consistency level based on application needs

### Transaction Isolation Levels

- __Read Uncommitted__: Lowest isolation, allows dirty reads
- __Read Committed__: Prevents dirty reads, allows non-repeatable reads
- __Repeatable Reads__: Prevents non-repeatable reads, allows phantom reads
- __Serializable__: Highest isolation, prevents all concurrency anomalies

## Communication Patterns

### Message Queues

- Asynchronous communication between components or services

- Benefits:

  - Decoupling sender from receiver
  - Reliability and fault tolerance
  - Load balancing and scalability
  - Buffering during traffic spikes

- __Models__:

  - Producer-Consumer: Basic message passing
  - Publish-Subscribe: Multiple consumers receive each message
  - Request-Reply: Bidirectional communication

- __Implementations__:

  - Kafka: Pull-based, high throughput, topic partitioning
  - RabbitMQ: Push-based, flexible routing

### Event-Driven Architecture

- Components communicate through events rather than direct calls
- More loosely coupled than request-response model
- Better suited for complex, distributed systems
- Excellent for real-time processing and reactive systems

## Security

### Authentication vs Authorization

- __Authentication__: Verifies who you are (identity)
  - Methods: Passwords, MFA, biometrics, certificates
- __Authorization__: Determines what you can do (permissions)
  - Methods: Role-based (RBAC), Attribute-based (ABAC), ACLs

### OAuth and Token-based Authentication

- __OAuth__: Delegates authentication to trusted third parties

  - Flow: Request → Grant → Token → Access
  - Benefits: No credential sharing, granular permissions, standardization

- __Token-based Authentication__:

  - JWT (JSON Web Token): Self-contained, stateless
  - Session tokens: Server-side, stateful
  - Trade-offs: Scalability vs revocation ease

## Practical System Design Examples

### File Storage System (Dropbox)

- __Key components__:

  - Metadata service: Stores file metadata in database
  - Storage service: Manages actual file content (often blob storage)
  - Synchronization service: Keeps files consistent across devices

- __Handling large files__:

  - Break into chunks (5-10MB)
  - Calculate fingerprints for each chunk and whole file
  - Use presigned URLs for direct-to-storage upload
  - Track upload status per chunk
  - Resume uploads from last successful chunk

### Content Streaming Platform (YouTube)

- __Video upload flow__:

  - Split videos into small segments
  - Transcode into multiple formats/resolutions
  - Create manifest file for adaptive streaming

- __Video streaming flow__:

  - Client fetches manifest file
  - Chooses format based on network conditions
  - Downloads segments sequentially
  - Adapts quality as network conditions change

- __Analytics system (Top-k videos)__:

  - Kafka/event streaming for view events
  - Sharded counting services by region
  - Aggregation service to combine regional data
  - Support for time-windowed queries

In real-world system design, these concepts are combined to create robust, scalable, and efficient systems that can handle millions or billions of users while maintaining performance and reliability. The art of system design is knowing which trade-offs to make based on your specific requirements and constraints.


## Advanced Data Structures and Algorithms

### HyperLogLog

- __Purpose__: Probabilistic data structure for approximating the number of unique elements (cardinality)

- __Advantage__: Requires significantly less memory than exact counting methods

- __How It Works__:

  - Uses hash functions to map elements to bit patterns
  - Counts the maximum number of consecutive trailing zeros in the binary representation
  - Intuitively, the probability of observing a pattern with k trailing zeros is 2^(-k)
  - Divides elements into buckets and averages observations for better accuracy
  - Gives approximation with typical error rate ~2%

- __Applications__:

  - Counting unique visitors to a website
  - Estimating unique search queries
  - Analyzing unique elements in large datasets without storing them

### Bloom Filters

- __Purpose__: Space-efficient probabilistic data structure for set membership testing

- __Properties__:

  - Can tell if an element is definitely not in a set
  - May give false positives (incorrectly say element is in set)
  - Never gives false negatives

- __How It Works__:

  - Uses multiple hash functions to set bits in a bit array
  - To check if element exists, checks if all corresponding bits are set

- __Applications__:

  - Preventing "one-hit wonders" in caches
  - Quickly filtering non-existent data before expensive lookups
  - Spell checkers and web crawlers

### Inverted Index

- __Purpose__: Data structure for full-text search

- __How It Works__:

  - Maps content (words) to their locations
  - Instead of searching documents for words, search words for documents

- __Example__:

  ```javascript
  Word      → Documents
  -----------------------
  "The"     → [1, 2, 3]
  "quick"   → [1]
  "brown"   → [1]
  "fox"     → [1, 3]
  ```

- __Applications__:

  - Search engines
  - Full-text search in databases
  - Content indexing systems

